      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-6c979cc65fb66878\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Prince Andrei’s direct “Я вас люблю” primarily avows as true his present love for Natasha. Its immediate effect is forgiving and reconciling, giving weak support to a relational undertaking, but it does not clearly renew their engagement or promise future conduct. The utterance is anticipated in his prior reflection and grounded in a carefully articulated conception of divine love, so it is not presented as a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The forgiving, reconciliatory setting gives slight performative pressure, but the narration explicitly leaves renewal of the betrothal unspoken.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Prince Andrei directly says “Я вас люблю” (“I love you”) to Natasha after she asks his forgiveness; this is the first of his two adjacent love avowals.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Mortally wounded and intermittently delirious, Andrei has nevertheless reflected with “perfect clarity” on a new, unconditional, divine love and has specifically wished for one more opportunity to see Natasha and speak to her. His avowal answers her plea for forgiveness and communicates forgiveness and reconciliation. The subsequent “I love you more, better than before” confirms the avowed state, while the narrator’s statement that neither of them discusses resuming their former engagement limits the case for performative recommitment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“— Я вас люблю, — сказал князь Андрей.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit, affirmative direct statement strongly supports truth-conditional avowal.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Before Natasha appears, Andrei thinks that he now experiences a blissful, pure, divine love and wishes he could see her once more and, looking into her eyes, say what he feels.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance expresses a consciously recognised and previously contemplated state rather than an unmediated verbal reflex.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Natasha repeatedly asks forgiveness; Andrei responds with the love avowal and then asks, “Чтò простить?” (“What is there to forgive?”).\",\"supports\":\"The avowal functions contextually as forgiveness and reconciliation, providing limited support for performative relational force.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Andrei follows with “Я люблю тебя больше, лучше чем прежде” (“I love you more, better than before”).\",\"supports\":\"The elaboration reinforces that he is avowing the character and degree of his love.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration says that although others imagine their former relationship as betrothed might resume if he recovers, neither Natasha nor Andrei speaks about it.\",\"supports\":\"This limits performative support: the love declaration does not clearly renew the engagement or establish a future undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the dominant avowal, with slight P support for its reconciliatory relational effect. Delivery and emotion are adequately treated as context, without independent E force or any residual need for O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"Classification targets the exact first occurrence “Я вас люблю,” not the immediately following expanded avowal.\"}"
        }
      ],
